“Lift us toward the sun,” whispered feather and drop, “and we will teach brightness to rest.”

Sela climbed the canyon path carrying the cup. At the highest ledge, where stones split from heat, she poured the drop over the feather and blew across the rim. A low note spread outward, dark and gentle.

The sunlight bent. Behind Sela unfolded the first shadow, soft as a wing and deep as a well. It cooled her feet, then stretched to trees, birds, children, and stones. The drop became a spring, singing below.

Now shadows travel with all beings, and echoes answer from hollow places. Elders say Sela’s cup still teaches the fierce sun mercy and the lonely earth reply.
