Myth: Before doorways knew welcome, every house faced inward. Villagers heard travelers calling beyond their walls, yet each threshold opened only to its own hearth, and strangers slept beneath cold stars.

Tavi, maker of shaded flutes, lived in a red-stone village where roads nearly met but turned away shyly. In his clay jar of dusk he kept a black feather, a silver shell, and one patient drop of water. One night they whispered, “Play for the closed places, and we will teach shelter to share its name.”

