Tavi, carver of shaded flutes, lived in a house whose doorway had once turned outward, teaching the village welcome. Yet he noticed that people opened their doors but not their hearts, speaking kindly while guarding their hidden storms. In his clay jar of dusk he kept a black feather, a silver shell, and one patient drop of water. At midnight they whispered, “Play for the covered eyes of houses, and we will teach seeing to become kindness.”

Tavi climbed to the highest shutter in the village. He set the shell upon his flute, tied the feather to the latch, and touched the drop to the dark pane. Then he played a braided song, soft as rain learning names, winding through hinges, roads, and sleeping bread ovens.

The shutters trembled. One by one, windows opened. Lamplight flowed out; starlight flowed in. Neighbors saw each other’s tears, mending, laughter, and empty bowls, and carried soup across thresholds before morning.

Since then, windows hold both reflection and horizon. Elders say Tavi’s tune hums in every pane, reminding eyes that true shelter begins when seeing becomes care.
